WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.110
The dramatic narratives of the geologic past are perfectly suited to the human

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:10.440
appetite for storytelling. Like many who
experienced childhood or parenthood in

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.700
the last 50 years, I loved Maurice
Sendak's 'Where the Wild Things Are.' It's

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.930
a book about the power of imagination to take us to other worlds, transcend time

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:24.119
and save us from our worst selves.
I think of Max's voyage whenever I teach

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:28.320
'History of Earth and Life,' a course in
which we cover Earth's 4 and 1/2 billion

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.640
year history in a 10-week term at an
average clip of about 450 million years

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.460
a week. I always feel that I've been on
a long journey with my students. We've

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:42.420
witnessed alien earths, explored
alternate ecosystems teeming with

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.469
strange wild things, through bountiful times and bad ones.

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:51.120
Finally, we approach the present and see
the modern world emerging from the mists

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.270
just as Max's room gradually sheds its
vines and becomes recognizable again. I

00:00:56.270 --> 00:01:01.559
believe, perhaps naively, that if more
people could experience this journey and

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:05.430
see themselves as earthlings with a common past and shared destiny it might

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.150
spring us out of our polarized and entrenched ways of thinking about the

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:15.240
world. As a geologist, I've worked with
people from all over the world, from very

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:20.100
different cultures and generations, older
and younger than mine. The strong human

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.549
connections I've developed with them through our shared study of earth make

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:28.110
me optimistic that a geocentric, timeful
worldview might help us overcome the

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.549
animosities and barriers that divide us
and get down to work on things that

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:35.610
really matter. Collective awareness of Earth's story

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:39.750
might save us from environmental hubris. Understanding how historical

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:43.799
happenstance is written into each of our personal lives might cause us to treat

00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:47.939
each other with more empathy. And a timeful habit of mine might even make us

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:52.590
less neurotic about the fact of our own
mortality by shifting our focus from the

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:56.579
finite lengths of our lives to the rich anthology of experiences that a lifetime

00:01:56.579 --> 00:02:01.799
represents. While other senses may be
dulled with age, the sense of time which

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:06.420
can only be developed by experiencing it, is heightened. Growing old requires one

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:10.849
to shed the illusion that there's only
one version of the world.

